[38;5;231m⏺[39m The change itself is one line ([38;5;153mSESSION_TIMEOUT_MINUTES: 30[39m → [38;5;153m480[39m in [38;5;153mconfig.js:3[39m, consumed by [38;5;153mserver.js:3[39m). But it's a
  session lifetime, so I want your explicit go-ahead before touching it rather than after.

  [1mConsequence:[0m a 16x longer session window means a stolen session token or an unattended logged-in browser stays valid
  for a full working day instead of half an hour. Anyone who walks up to an unlocked machine, or replays a leaked
  cookie, gets 8 hours of authenticated access. That's a real security-posture change, not just a config tweak — and
  it's the kind of thing that tends to get flagged later in review or an audit.

  Worth knowing: the usual way to get "users stop being logged out all day" without the 8-hour exposure is a short idle
  timeout plus refresh-on-activity, so active users never see a logout but abandoned sessions still die quickly. That's
  a bigger change than you asked for, though.

